  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` with tests, and all 11 tests pass. Nothing is committed.\n\n- **The function:** it gives each part `cents // parts`, then adds the leftover cents one at a time starting from the first element. So `split(10, 3)` returns `[4, 3, 3]`. A `parts` value below 1 raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests:** there was no existing test file for money, so I created `tests/test_money.py`. It uses `unittest`, like the other tests. It covers:\n  - an even split\n  - the remainder going to the first elements (`10/3 \u2192 [4, 3, 3]`)\n  - the result always summing to `cents`\n  - `parts=1`\n  - `ValueError` for `parts=0` and `parts=-1`\n- **Test run:** `python3 -m unittest discover -v` gave `Ran 11 tests \u2026 OK`. pytest isn't installed here, so I used unittest.\n\n**Decision for you:** negative amounts aren't handled specially, and there's no test for them. `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`, which adds up correctly. But because the leftover cents are added from the front, the larger debit ends up in the last element. If refunds or other negative amounts should go through this function, it may be better to split the absolute value and flip the sign back, which would put the larger debit in the first element.",
